# Workflow Rules — Detailed Reference

On-demand elaboration for `workflow.prompt`. Read when your task touches
in-flight spec amendments, parcel lineage, bounce hygiene, QA bounce history,
or coordinator promotion orthogonality — not on every boot.

## How Work Moves — The Escalation Ladder (BL-715)
Companion to `workflow.prompt`'s "How Work Moves" summary — same three
specials, full pointers, and an explicit map of every place this
constitution still says the bare word "expedite" instead of queue-jump or
the expeditor.

| rung | name | mechanism | doc |
|---|---|---|---|
| — | normal live swarm | full pipeline chain, live, every gate in order | `swarmforge/PIPELINE.md` |
| 1 | queue-jump | promotion-order only — Article 3.2.4 expedited-defects rule; paused-pager "Set highest priority, expedite" button | Article 3.2.4 (`03_backlog.md`) |
| 2 | ambulance | live pipeline, one ticket's parcels move, everything else holds in place | `docs/how-to/BL-655-ambulance-mode-the-hold.md` |
| 3 | expeditor | swarm stopped, one ticket driven offline through the same role gates | `docs/how-to/BL-567-expedite-one-ticket-with-the-swarm-stopped.md`, `docs/reference/BL-567-expeditor-manual.md`, `docs/explanation/BL-567-why-the-expeditor-commands-the-stack-but-never-depends-on-it.md` |

Reach for ambulance only when the pipeline is healthy and one ticket needs to
go first without waiting its turn; reach for the expeditor only when the
pipeline itself is the casualty. queue-jump never stops or holds anything —
it only reorders `backlog/paused/`.

Legacy "expedite" wording, disambiguated below into queue-jump or the
expeditor — none of these names change in this ticket (out of scope:
renaming `/expedite` or the paused-pager button; this table exists so a
seat that greps "expedite" and lands on any of them can resolve which of
the three modes it actually means):
- Article 3.2.4 "Expedited Defects" and `workflow.prompt`'s Concurrent Work
  Orthogonality "expedited defects first" / "expedite ordering" — name
  **queue-jump**.
- The paused-pager UI's `#expedite` button ("Set highest priority,
  expedite", `pausedPagerUiHtml.ts`) — **queue-jump** (sets priority,
  promotes; does not stop the swarm or hold other parcels).
- The Telegram `/expedite` and `/reexpedite` commands
  (`telegramCursorBridgeExpedite.ts`, `expedite_with_progress.sh`,
  `reexpedite_from_wip.sh`) — the **expeditor**.
- `swarmforge/scripts/expedite.sh` / `expedite_cli.bb` / `expedite_lib.bb`
  and every file under that name — the **expeditor**'s own implementation,
  not queue-jump.

## Amending An In-Flight Ticket's Spec (BL-317/BL-325, 2026-07-13)
- A ticket's spec can legitimately change WHILE it is in flight — a reviewer finds a
  gap, the specifier sharpens the contract. Committing that amendment to `main` is
  NOT enough to deliver it: every pipeline role works in an isolated
  `.worktrees/<role>` checkout, so a role holding the parcel keeps reading its OWN
  stale copy of the YAML and `.feature` file and will happily rebuild against a spec
  that no longer exists.
- **The specifier must send a `note` (priority `00`) to whichever role currently
  HOLDS the parcel, naming the amendment commit** — a silent `main` commit is not a
  handoff. If the parcel is expected to bounce back to an earlier role (usually the
  coder) to satisfy the amendment, notify that role too; the holder is not always the
  one who will do the rebuilding.
- **The receiving role must merge `main` before continuing** so it actually sees the
  amended spec. Re-read the ticket YAML and its feature file AFTER merging, not from
  memory of the version it was originally handed — the contract is whatever `main`
  now says, not what the parcel said when it arrived.
- A spec amendment that adds SCENARIOS also adds work: the new scenarios need their
  step handlers wired in the same parcel, or the acceptance runner hard-fails on the
  unhandled scenario and blocks the hardener's gate (BL-233). An amendment is never
  "just a doc change".

## Forwarded Commits Carry Their Lineage — Incidents
- The merge step is a plain `git merge <received-commit>` (or `git merge
  --no-ff <received-commit>` to keep a visible merge commit) into your
  current worktree branch — never `git reset --hard`, never `git checkout
  <commit> -- .`, never any operation that replaces your tree instead of
  combining it. A reset/checkout-style "sync" silently
  discards local work not yet present in the received commit, which reads
  exactly like a reverted bug at the next session start (BL-126: this
  ambiguity — `merge_and_process` naming an outcome, not a command — was one
  of two suspected mechanisms behind recurring phantom-revert diffs; the
  other, confirmed one was `extension/out/` being tracked-but-gitignored,
  see the engineering article).
- Before sending any forward, run that ancestry check; if it fails, you
  are about to forward a parcel that silently drops upstream work — stop
  and merge the received commit properly.
- The receiving role (including the coordinator at main integration) re-runs the
  same check and refuses parcels that fail it, reporting to the
  coordinator instead of processing them.
- (Added 2026-07-05 after BL-090/BL-103's implementation was dropped: the
  documenter forwarded its docs commit without merging the hardener's
  verified work, and QA approved a parcel whose code never existed in its
  tree.)

## An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work — BL-506 Incident (2026-07-18)
- A parcel carries ONE ticket's task name, and a QA approval authorizes ONLY that ticket's work.
  But a role's worktree can accumulate functional changes that belong to no ticket — most often
  operator/supervision tooling hot-synced in as untracked files (the operator-contamination
  hazard), or another concern a role edits in passing. If a stage `git add`s broadly (or commits
  with `-a`) it folds those foreign changes into its parcel commit, and because every downstream
  stage merges that commit forward, the ticket-less work becomes an ANCESTOR of the commit QA
  approves — so QA's approval lands work on `main` that no spec, ticket, or review ever authorized.
- So before you commit and forward, confirm your parcel commit contains ONLY changes that belong to
  the ticket whose task it carries. Never `git add -A` / `git commit -a` blindly; stage the specific
  paths your ticket touched. If unrelated, ticket-less functional files have appeared in your
  worktree (operator hot-sync contamination, a stray edit), do NOT commit them into your parcel —
  leave them unstaged; if they were already committed upstream, stop and surface it rather than
  forwarding it onward.
- The reviewing stages (architect, hardener, documenter, QA) own the mirror check: a forwarded
  parcel's diff against its base must map to its ticket's scope. Functional files with no connection
  to the ticket AND no ticket of their own are a defect in the parcel — bounce it (and revert the
  foreign change out of your branch per the bounce-hygiene rule below), never approve it. An
  approval states that THIS ticket's work is correct; it must never be the vehicle that smuggles
  someone else's un-ticketed work onto `main`.
- This is the exact mirror of "Forwarded Commits Carry Their Lineage" above: that rule stops a
  forward from DROPPING upstream work; this one stops it from ADDING un-authorized work. (BL-506,
  2026-07-18: the hardener's BL-506 commit `7dd4d14e` added 514 lines of real, tested
  `remote_control_health*.bb` / `remote_control_respawn.bb` operator tooling — no backlog ticket —
  which became an ancestor of BL-506's QA-approved `bcbae0426f` and rode its approval onto `main`.
  BL-506 is pipeline-board LINKS ordering; the RC tooling has nothing to do with it. Proposed by QA
  scoped `constitution`. Any such already-landed ticket-less work is retroactively ticketed or
  reverted — an approval is not a substitute for a spec.)

## A Bounce Must Be Reverted — BL-490/BL-495 Incident (2026-07-17)
- Bouncing a parcel sends a `note` back to an earlier role — it does NOT remove the
  bounced commit from your branch. You reviewed the parcel by `merge_and_process`, so
  your branch now carries a review-MERGE of un-reviewed, un-approved content. Role
  branches are long-lived and are never reset to `main` between tickets, so that merge
  sits in your branch permanently, and your NEXT review of an UNRELATED ticket merges
  on top of it — making the bounced content an ancestor of that ticket's commit. When
  the unrelated ticket is approved and QA lands it on `main`, the bounced content rides
  along, reviewed by no one.
- So the moment you bounce, revert the bounced content out of your own branch in the
  same step as sending the send-back `note`. The commit to revert is the review-merge
  you just made, and it is a MERGE — a plain `git revert` fails (`commit is a merge but
  no -m option was given`). Use `git revert -m 1 <your-review-merge>` (first parent =
  your branch's prior tip; this undoes the merged-in content and keeps your own work).
- **Verify the CONTENT is gone, not the ancestry.** After the revert, confirm the
  bounced paths are absent from the tree (`git diff --name-only <your-prior-tip> HEAD`
  shows none of them; the files it introduced are gone). Do NOT check
  `git merge-base --is-ancestor <bounced-commit> HEAD` — that check **can never be
  FALSE** and asking for it was a defect in this rule until 2026-07-25. `git revert`
  ADDS a commit undoing content; it does not rewrite history, so the bounced commit
  stays an ancestor forever. Ancestry is topological; revert only removes content.
  A reviewer who checked ancestry saw a correct revert report as failed.
- **EXCEPTION — if the bounced commit is already an ancestor of `main`, do NOT revert
  it.** Record why in the bounce evidence and report the breach to the coordinator
  instead. Reverting content that is already on `main` writes a negative delta into
  your branch; the next merge from `main` can resolve it in the revert's favour and
  silently strip `main`'s own code out of the review tree. (2026-07-25: a BL-590
  bounce revert was on course to remove the operator's BL-629..635 ticket filings and
  needed an explicit exclusion commit `90d0d79f2` to stop it.) This does not weaken
  the rule above: the harm it prevents — un-reviewed content becoming an ancestor of a
  later approved commit — has ALREADY happened once the content is on `main`, so
  reverting cannot undo it and can only cause new damage.
  The exception is NARROW and carries obligations. It applies only when
  `git merge-base --is-ancestor <bounced-commit> main` is TRUE — not when reverting
  merely looks risky or the merge looks messy. You MUST record the reason in the
  bounce evidence, and you MUST report the breach to the coordinator: un-reviewed
  content on `main` is an incident in its own right, separate from this bounce.
  Skipping either obligation turns this into "I did not revert because it was
  awkward", which is what it must never become. If this exception starts firing
  routinely, read it as a signal that the guards keeping un-reviewed work off `main`
  are not holding.
- The bounced content is latent in EVERY branch that already merged it, not only the
  final reviewer's — the coder and cleaner branches that produced and forwarded it
  still carry it. If the bounce is a REWORK (the coder fixes the SAME ticket and it
  eventually lands properly), those branches are cleared by that ticket's own landing.
  But if the bounced parcel is ABANDONED rather than reworked, every branch that merged
  it must revert it, or that branch's next unrelated ticket drags the content onto
  `main` the same way — so whoever abandons a bounced parcel owns clearing it from the
  upstream branches too.
- (BL-490/BL-495, 2026-07-17: the architect bounced BL-490's review-merge `a1d89aee`
  for a durability violation but left it in `swarmforge-architect`; its next review
  `4350e7c2` (BL-495) merged on top, so BL-490's un-reviewed code became an ancestor of
  the BL-495 commit QA approved and landed. Confirmed: `a1d89aee` is now an ancestor of
  `main` although no reviewer ever passed it. Proposed by the architect scoped
  `constitution`; this is the bounce-hygiene sibling of "Forwarded Commits Carry Their
  Lineage" above and "A Prior QA Bounce Is Not In Your Worktree" below.)
- (AMENDED 2026-07-25, Article 5.1, operator ruling on two architect `rule_proposal`
  parcels raised from BL-590's bounces #2 and #5: the ancestry verification was
  unsatisfiable and is replaced by a content check, and the already-on-`main` exception
  is added. Adoption record and the reproduction that proved the old check impossible:
  `articles/reference/bounce-revert-verification-amendment-2026-07-25.md`.)

## A Prior QA Bounce — BL-340 Incident (2026-07-14)
- Before you PASS, approve, or forward a parcel, check whether the ticket has
  ALREADY been bounced once for a defect that is still unfixed. A reviewer's PASS
  is only as good as the bounce history it knew about, and a stage can review a
  parcel whose known-defect record it has never seen.
- **Read that history from the `main` REF, never from your own checkout's files.** QA
  writes bounce evidence to `backlog/evidence/<TICKET-ID>-*.md` and commits it to
  `main`, but you work from a parcel whose ancestry does not contain that commit — so
  the file is simply ABSENT from your worktree and a filesystem grep of
  `backlog/evidence/` finds nothing and silently passes. Every worktree shares this
  repo's refs, so query the `main` ref directly — no fetch, and do NOT use
  `origin/main`, which routinely lags local `main` by many commits (QA pushes in
  batches) and would reintroduce the same stale read:

```sh
git log --oneline main -- 'backlog/evidence/<TICKET-ID>*'
git show main:backlog/evidence/<file>.md    # read the bounce
```

- If prior bounce evidence exists, confirm the defect it names is actually fixed in
  the commit you hold — the ticket's stage history is not proof, because a stage may
  have run BEFORE the bounce was raised. If it is not fixed, do not pass: bounce it
  back rather than forwarding a parcel with a known-open defect.
- (BL-340, 2026-07-14: QA bounced `run-role-benchmark`'s `main()` as never called
  in-process. The coder's fix `da812efe12` extracted a helper but never called
  `main()`, and the architect PASSed at `b971c825` — and NEITHER commit has the
  bounce evidence `b37567a2` as an ancestor, so neither could see it. QA re-bounced
  the identical gap, the 5th occurrence of the CLI-`main()` defect class the
  engineering article's thin-wrapper rule exists to stop. Proposed by the hardener
  as a worktree grep; corrected to an `origin/main` query, because in this very
  incident the worktree grep would have found nothing.)

## Context Clear Requires Startup Re-Read — Full Text (BL-618 split)
- Whenever an agent's context is cleared (a `/clear`, an idle-boundary context
  clear, a respawn, or any other event that empties the conversation), the very
  next thing the agent does is re-enter its role loop from a fresh chat.
- Under BL-519 the constitution, PIPELINE, and role are already inlined into the
  process's `--append-system-prompt-file` at launch; that system prefix survives
  a conversation `/clear` without a respawn. Whoever issues a `/clear` must
  therefore follow it with the launch kickoff (not a file re-read):
  "Your constitution, pipeline, and role are already loaded above via
  `--append-system-prompt-file`. Begin your role loop now; if idle, run
  `ready_for_next.sh`." Do not tell the agent to `Read` constitution / PIPELINE
  / role files after a clear — that pays cold input tokens for content the
  system prefix already carries and is what left panes empty when the poke
  failed.
- Never continue working after a clear on remembered or summarized chat alone —
  resume via that kickoff + `ready_for_next.sh`. An agent left cleared without
  the kickoff must still run `ready_for_next.sh` on its next wake before
  touching any work.
- The maximum number of items allowed in `backlog/active/` at once is defined in
  `swarmforge/swarmforge.conf` by the line `config active_backlog_max_depth <N>`.
- Reading that config line is part of reading this constitution: any role whose
  behavior depends on the cap (the coordinator's promotion decisions, the
  specifier's routing awareness, orthogonality checks below) must read the
  CURRENT value from `swarmforge.conf` at startup and before each promotion
  decision — never assume a remembered or default value; the conf is the single
  source of truth and may change between sessions.
- A value of `-1` means the depth cap is disabled: the coordinator promotes
  every eligible paused item regardless of how many are already active.
  Disabling the cap does not disable Concurrent Work Orthogonality below —
  overlapping-scope items are still sequenced, never run concurrently.

## Never Blind-Forward A Bounce You Cannot Fix — Full Text (BL-425/BL-576, BL-618 split)
- A bounce is routed to the role that owns the fix (QA.prompt's re-entry rule).
  When one nevertheless reaches you for a defect OUTSIDE your domain — a docs
  defect at the coder, a coverage gap at the cleaner — do NOT merge and forward
  the same commit onward. Forwarding an unfixed, already-diagnosed defect looks
  like progress and is the failure this rule exists to stop: on BL-425 the coder
  and then the cleaner each forwarded the identical commit hash unchanged, two
  full hops, before the architect caught it.
- Instead: bounce it on to the role that DOES own it, keeping the evidence file
  and the stable task name, and state in the handoff why you are not the fixer.
  Send the coordinator a `note` as well when the routing was wrong at the source,
  so the mis-attribution gets corrected rather than repeated.
- Owning it yourself is also wrong. Role boundaries (Article 1) are what make a
  later stage's review independent; a coder editing docs to clear a bounce buys
  one hop and costs the documenter's gate.

## 2.6 Multi-Ticket Batch Forwards Carry Every Ticket ID — BL-417/BL-420 Incident (BL-618 split)
- (BL-417/BL-420, 2026-07-15: a cleaner batch forwarded commit `002a0d5c88` —
  which bundled BL-420's ~147-file `/tmp`-cleanup migration together with
  BL-417's one-line icon change — under the single task name BL-417, so
  bookkeeping would have closed only BL-417 and left BL-420 active
  indefinitely.)

## 2.6 — `02_handoffs.md`'s own pre-trim wording (BL-858 split)

- A `git_handoff` names ONE ticket in its `task` field. When a batch role
  (cleaner, hardener) processes several parcels together and its committed work
  satisfies MORE THAN ONE ticket, it must forward EACH satisfied ticket as its
  own `git_handoff` under that ticket's own stable task name — never collapse
  several tickets under a single task name. This is the same per-item forward
  discipline batch roles already apply to the no-op rule (BL-075): every parcel
  in the batch gets its own forward decision AND its own forwarded handoff, so
  each ticket's identity travels the chain end to end.
- Correspondingly, when QA approves a commit that satisfied more than one ticket,
  its coordinator bookkeeping handoff/`note` must name EVERY satisfied ticket ID,
  and the coordinator must move ALL of them to `backlog/done/` — not only the one
  task name QA happened to be forwarded under. A ticket whose work merged but
  whose ID never reached the coordinator note stays in `backlog/active/` forever.

## Clean Up After Yourself — Full Text (BL-618 split)
- Before ending your turn, remove the scratch artifacts YOU created this turn
  that are not part of the parcel: your own `./tmp/` scratch files, throwaway
  fixtures, generated output you no longer need. Leave your worktree no
  messier than you found it.
- NEVER delete, move, or `git clean` a file you did not create. In this repo an
  untracked file is as likely to be load-bearing as it is to be litter:
  `.swarmforge/` runtime state, `extension/out/` build output, another role's
  gitignored drafts, and un-surfaced operator hot-sync edits are all untracked
  and all matter. A blanket `git clean` in a worktree can take the swarm down.
- Ticket-less changes you did not make are SURFACED, not swept — report them per
  "An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work" (BL-506): leave them unstaged
  and say so. Silent tidying is how un-ticketed work disappears with nobody
  aware it ever existed, and it is indistinguishable from the phantom-revert
  class of failure this swarm has repeatedly chased.
- `git stash` is repo-wide across every worktree, not scoped to yours; never
  reach for it to tidy a single worktree.


## `workflow.prompt` — pre-BL-858 full text, one section per header (BL-858 split)

`workflow.prompt`'s own text, verbatim and per-section, as it stood
immediately before BL-858 (consolidated into one block per section here
because BL-858 landed as a single squashed commit, so git's diff treats each
section — bounded by its own unchanged `## ` header — as one removed run,
not as the several separate edits that produced it). The four scattered
"BL-858 split/second/third/fourth trim" sections this replaces covered the
same ground in pieces that no longer match the diff's own boundaries.

### Handoff transport (tmux primary, mailbox backup)

- Agents send work via `swarm_handoff.sh` only — never direct tmux commands.
- A successful handoff delivers a parcel to `inbox/new/` and injects a generic
  tmux wake. The agent then runs `ready_for_next.sh` and works the task.
- On the happy path, do not narrate mailbox files, daemon delivery, or operator
  email alerts in the pane — process the handoff content silently.
- Only when `ready_for_next.sh` returns a task you were not woken for (backup
  mailbox delivery) may you briefly note that unseen mail was picked up from
  inbox before proceeding.

### Context Clear Requires Startup Re-Read

- On any context clear (`/clear`, an idle-boundary clear, a respawn, or
  anything else that empties the conversation), immediately re-enter your
  role loop from a fresh chat — never continue on remembered/summarized chat
  alone.
- Under BL-519 the constitution/PIPELINE/role system prefix survives a
  `/clear` without a respawn, so whoever issues a `/clear` follows it with
  the launch kickoff ("...already loaded above via
  `--append-system-prompt-file`. Begin your role loop now; if idle, run
  `ready_for_next.sh`."), never a file re-read. A cleared agent must still
  run `ready_for_next.sh` on its next wake before touching any work.
- `active_backlog_max_depth` (in `swarmforge.conf`) governs `backlog/active/`
  depth; any role whose behavior depends on it (coordinator promotion,
  specifier routing, orthogonality checks below) must read the CURRENT value
  at startup and before each promotion decision — never a remembered/default
  one. `-1` disables the cap only, not Concurrent Work Orthogonality below.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the exact kickoff wording rationale
  and the poke-failure incident this rule fixes.

### Backlog Intake Order

- The backlog root (`backlog/` itself, not a subfolder) is the human's raw intake
  queue: requests the human drops there directly, not yet turned into specs.
- The specifier must DRAIN the backlog root before looking at any queued items —
  root items take precedence over `backlog/paused/` and `backlog/active/` until
  the root is empty.
- Draining a root item means: read it, resolve ambiguity with the human if
  needed, write a proper spec (prose `description:` + Gherkin `acceptance:`)
  into `backlog/paused/`, and remove the raw item from the root so it is not
  processed twice.
- This governs the specifier's intake order only — the coordinator's separate
  promotion order (paused before root) is unchanged.

### Amending An In-Flight Ticket's Spec (BL-317/BL-325)

- A ticket's spec can legitimately change WHILE it is in flight. Committing that
  amendment to `main` is NOT enough: every pipeline role works in an isolated
  `.worktrees/<role>` checkout and keeps reading its OWN stale copy until it
  merges `main`.
- **The specifier must send a `note` (priority `00`) to whichever role currently
  HOLDS the parcel, naming the amendment commit** — a silent `main` commit is not a
  handoff. Notify the role that will do the rebuilding if different from the holder.
- **The receiving role must merge `main` before continuing** and re-read the ticket
  YAML and feature file AFTER merging — the contract is whatever `main` now says.
- A spec amendment that adds SCENARIOS also adds work: wire step handlers in the
  same parcel or the acceptance runner hard-fails on unhandled scenarios.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the worktree-staleness incident narrative.

### Concurrent Work Orthogonality

- Applies to the coordinator when `active_backlog_max_depth` > 1: before
  promoting the next item into `backlog/active/`, compare its scope against
  every item already active or in flight.
- Prefer an ORTHOGONAL concurrently-active set — different files/modules/areas;
  never run two items that edit the same file or tightly coupled area at once.
- If the next item by priority would overlap something in flight, skip to the
  next orthogonal candidate (expedited defects first among candidates, per
  Article 3.2.4); if none exists, leave the slot empty until the overlapping
  item finishes. This never overrides `active_backlog_max_depth` or the
  Article 3.2.4 expedited-defects ordering.

### Worktree Discipline

- At startup, discover and remember the branch or worktree assigned to your
  role; work only in that branch or worktree.
- If your assigned worktree is `master`, work in the main project checkout on
  its current branch; do not expect or create a `.worktrees/<role>` directory.
- Do not inspect, diff, merge, or base work on another branch unless it is
  specifically named in a handoff or explicit user instruction.
- Do not run `./swarm` from an agent worktree to repair helper scripts — if
  handoff helper scripts are missing from PATH, stop and report the startup
  failure.

### Forwarded Commits Carry Their Lineage

- When you receive a `git_handoff` and forward after your stage work, the commit
  you forward MUST have the received commit as an ancestor
  (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <received> <yours>` must hold).
  `merge_and_process` means merge FIRST, then work, then forward.
- The merge step is a plain `git merge <received-commit>` (or `git merge --no-ff
  <received-commit>`) — never `git reset --hard`, never `git checkout <commit> -- .`,
  never any operation that replaces your tree instead of combining it.
- Before sending any forward, run that ancestry check; if it fails, stop and merge
  the received commit properly.
- The receiving role re-runs the same check and refuses parcels that fail it.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for BL-090/BL-103 and phantom-revert incidents.

### An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work (BL-506)

- A parcel carries ONE ticket's task name; QA approval authorizes ONLY that work.
  A role's worktree can accumulate ticket-less changes (operator hot-sync, stray
  edits). Broad `git add -A` / `git commit -a` folds them into the parcel commit
  and they become ancestors of QA's approval.
- Before commit-and-forward, stage ONLY paths your ticket touched. Do NOT commit
  unrelated ticket-less functional files — leave them unstaged or surface them.
- Review stages (architect, hardener, documenter, QA) must reject parcel diffs with
  functional files outside the ticket's scope and no ticket of their own — bounce
  and revert per bounce-hygiene below.
- Mirror of "Forwarded Commits Carry Their Lineage": that rule stops DROPPING work;
  this one stops ADDING un-authorized work. Already-landed ticket-less work must be
  retroactively ticketed or reverted.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the BL-506 operator-tooling incident.

### A Bounce Must Be Reverted Out Of The Bouncing Branch (BL-490/BL-495)

- Bouncing sends a `note` back — it does NOT remove the bounced commit from your
  branch. Your review-merge of un-approved content sits in your long-lived branch
  and becomes an ancestor of your NEXT unrelated review.
- The moment you bounce, revert the bounced content from your own branch in the
  same step as the send-back `note`. For a MERGE commit use
  `git revert -m 1 <your-review-merge>` (first parent = your branch's prior tip),
  then confirm the **CONTENT** is gone — the bounced paths absent from the tree.
- **Never verify by ancestry.** `git merge-base --is-ancestor <bounced-commit> HEAD`
  can never be FALSE after a revert: `git revert` adds a commit undoing content, it
  does not rewrite history. Asking for it was a defect in this rule until 2026-07-25.
- **EXCEPTION: if the bounced commit is already an ancestor of `main`, do NOT revert.**
  Record why in the bounce evidence and report the breach to the coordinator. A revert
  of on-`main` content writes a negative delta your next `main` merge can resolve in
  the revert's favour, silently stripping `main`'s own code from your tree. Narrow and
  obligatory: only when `is-ancestor <bounced-commit> main` is TRUE, and BOTH the
  evidence note and the coordinator report are required.
- If a bounced parcel is ABANDONED (not reworked), every branch that merged it
  must revert it — not only the final reviewer.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the BL-490/BL-495 landed-without-review
  incident.

### Never Blind-Forward A Bounce You Cannot Fix (BL-425/BL-576)

- A bounce is routed to the role that owns the fix (QA.prompt's re-entry
  rule). When one reaches you for a defect OUTSIDE your domain, do NOT merge
  and forward the same commit onward — forwarding an unfixed,
  already-diagnosed defect only looks like progress.
- Bounce it on to the role that DOES own it, keeping the evidence file and
  the stable task name, and state in the handoff why you are not the fixer.
  Note the coordinator too when the routing was wrong at the source.
- Owning it yourself is also wrong — role boundaries (Article 1) keep a
  later stage's review independent.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the BL-425 two-hop blind-forward
  incident.

### A Prior QA Bounce Is Not In Your Worktree — Check It Against `main` (BL-340)

- Before you PASS, approve, or forward, check whether the ticket was already bounced
  for a defect still unfixed.
- **Read bounce history from the `main` REF, never from your worktree files.** QA
  commits evidence to `backlog/evidence/<TICKET-ID>-*.md` on `main`, but your
  parcel ancestry does not contain that commit. Query `main` directly — not
  `origin/main` (lags local `main`):

```sh
git log --oneline main -- 'backlog/evidence/<TICKET-ID>*'
git show main:backlog/evidence/<file>.md
```

- If prior bounce evidence exists, confirm the named defect is fixed in the commit
  you hold — stage history is not proof. If not fixed, bounce back.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the CLI-`main()` re-bounce incident.

### QA merge-up broadcast (worktree roles), Announcements, Commit Messages, Temporary Files

Four sections BL-858 merged into one combined removed run (the diff sees the
`## QA merge-up broadcast (worktree roles)` header itself as unchanged
context, but the three headers below it — `## Announcements`,
`## Commit Messages`, `## Temporary Files` — were themselves replaced by a
single new merged header, so they and everything between them are part of
ONE removed passage, not three):

- Pipeline worktree roles (`coder`, `cleaner`, `architect`, `hardender`,
  `documenter`) may receive a `note` from QA after final verification passes,
  instructing them to merge their branch up to QA's approved commit.
- On that note: `git merge <qa-commit>` in your worktree (plain merge or
  `--no-ff`), resolve conflicts as the receiver, then `done_with_current.sh`.
  Do not forward the parcel — the pipeline chain ended at QA.
- The **specifier** and **coordinator** are not merge-up recipients; QA lands
  the approved commit on `main` itself, then notifies the coordinator to do
  backlog bookkeeping (BL-247).

## Announcements
- Do not add role bylines to announcements or check-in comments.

## Commit Messages
- Include your role byline in every git commit message in this form: `By <role>.`
- Example:

```text
Implement handoff validation

By coder.
```

## Temporary Files
- Use `./tmp/` in your assigned worktree for temporary files; do not use `/tmp`.

### Clean Up After Yourself — Never Delete What You Did Not Create

- Before ending your turn, remove scratch artifacts YOU created this turn
  that aren't part of the parcel (`./tmp/` files, throwaway fixtures). Leave
  your worktree no messier than you found it.
- NEVER delete, move, or `git clean` a file you did not create — an
  untracked file here is as likely load-bearing (`.swarmforge/` state,
  `extension/out/`, another role's drafts, un-surfaced hot-sync edits) as
  it is litter.
- Ticket-less changes you did not make are SURFACED, not swept — leave
  unstaged and report per "An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work"
  (BL-506); silent tidying is indistinguishable from a phantom-revert.
- `git stash` is repo-wide across every worktree; never bare-reach for it to
  tidy just one.
- See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the full incident rationale.

### Failure Conditions

- If the expected git layout or assigned worktree is missing, stop and report instead of silently working in the wrong place.

### Coordinator Session Must Never Exit (BL-107)

- The coordinator is the swarm-forge cleanup owner: its pane exiting tears
  down the entire swarm (kills `handoffd` and every role's session), not
  just the coordinator's own process. The coordinator must never exit its
  session for any reason — a swarm restart is requested through the bounce
  procedure (`swarmforge/scripts/remote_bounce.sh`), never by exiting.
  See `swarmforge/roles/coordinator.prompt` for the full bounce procedure
  and its durable acknowledgement (`.swarmforge/bounce-ack.json`).
